  <qti-item-body>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="show_interaction" outcome-identifier="INTERACTION_VISIBILITY" show-hide="show" class="qti-margin-0">
      <qti-content-body>
        <p>Which sentence uses &quot;suffrage&quot; correctly?</p>
    <qti-choice-interaction response-identifier="RESPONSE" shuffle="true" max-choices="1">
      <qti-simple-choice identifier="A">Grandfather still called the drought years the family&apos;s long suffrage, when the well ran to mud and the cattle were sold off one by one at auction.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="B">By 1915 the league&apos;s suffrage caravans had crossed three states, registering supporters and lobbying every legislator along the route.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="C">The chef&apos;s ambitious new tasting menu earned the suffrage of the whole dining room, and the applause carried all the way back into the kitchen.</qti-simple-choice><qti-simple-choice identifier="D">Poll workers counted each suffrage by hand at the long table, unfolding the creased papers under the green lamps until well past midnight.</qti-simple-choice>
    </qti-choice-interaction>
      </qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>

    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-A" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! Suffrage means the right to vote, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because drought, mud, and lost cattle are hardship — the card&apos;s warning is that the resemblance to suffering is pure accident, with no shared meaning behind it.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-B" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Correct! Suffrage means the right to vote, so it&apos;s the correct answer because the caravans are campaigning for exactly that right — registering supporters and lobbying lawmakers to extend the ballot.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-C" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! Suffrage means the right to vote in political elections, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because applause for a tasting menu is approval — a delighted dining room grants a chef its admiration, not a legal right anyone can exercise.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-D" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! Suffrage means the right itself, so it&apos;s not the correct choice here because what the poll workers unfold and count are the paper ballots — suffrage is what entitled each voter to mark one, not the papers on the table.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
  </qti-item-body>
  <qti-response-processing>
